{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2669 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c4f594a63d19714599d25958fd690cb5335cea3e7b6d9a01277bf4fe5e9c0095" :edb/db-before-t 2668 :edb/db-after-t 2669 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535981 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:13:37.159Z" 13194139535981 true] [17592186179451 1100 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[4, 6]$\n\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 6]$\n\nCalculate  $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j,$ where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}.$" 13194139535981 true] [17592186179451 1100 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[4, 6]$\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 6]$\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j$, where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}$." 13194139535981 false] [17592186179451 1145 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nis just the length of the interval $[2, 6]$. So, we have\n\n$$\n∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j} = 6 - 2 = 4\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i$, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)(x_{i} + x_{i - 1})Δx_{i} &= (x_{1} + x_{0})Δx_{1} + (x_{2} + x_{1})Δx_{2} + ⋯ + (x_{m} + x_{m - 1})Δx_{n} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{0})(x_{1} - x_{0}) + (x_{2} + x_{1})(x_{2} - x_{1}) + (x_{m} + x_{m - 1})(x_{m} - x_{m - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} - x_{0}^{2}) + (x_{2}^{2} - x_{1}^{2}) + ⋯ + (x_{m}^{2} - x_{m - 1}^{2}) \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} - x_{0}^{2}) + (x_{2}^{2} - x_{1}^{2}) + ⋯ + (x_{m}^{2} - x_{m - 1}^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x_{0}^{2} + x_{m}^{2} \\\\\n&= x_{m}^{2} - x_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j = 20\\cdot 4 = 80\n$$" 13194139535981 false] [17592186179451 1145 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j$ is just the length of the interval $[2, 6].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=6-2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{m} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}\\,+\\,{x}_{0})({x}_{1}\\,-\\,{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}\\,+\\,{x}_{1})({x}_{2}\\,-\\,{x}_{1})+({x}_{m}\\,+\\,{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}\\,-\\,{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j = 20\\cdot 4 = 80.\n$$" 13194139535981 true] [17592186179514 1100 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1, 2]$\n\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 3]$\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n {3} \\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j,$ where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}.$" 13194139535981 true] [17592186179514 1100 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1, 2]$\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 3]$\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n {3} \\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j$, where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}$." 13194139535981 false] [17592186179514 1145 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)3Δx_{i}Δy_{j} &= 3∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\n&= 3∑_(i = 1)^(m)Δx_{i}∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\Delta x_i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nare just the lengths of the intervals $[-1, 2]$ and $[2,3]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)Δx_{i} &= 2 - (-1) = 3, \\\\\n∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j} &= 3 - 2 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sum_{i=1}^m \\Delta x_i \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 = 9\n$$" 13194139535981 false] [17592186179514 1145 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\Delta x_i$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[-1, 2]$ and $[2,3]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-(-1)=3, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sum_{i=1}^m \\Delta x_i \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 = 9.\n$$" 13194139535981 true] [17592186179596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378073 13194139535981 true] [17592186179596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378087 13194139535981 tru